IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `claq-dates`
IMPORT `claq-common`

-- Civil Liability Act 2003 (Queensland) -- chapter 3 (assessment of damages for personal injury) and chapter 4, part 2
-- (no jury), as the CLAIMANT meets them, with the numbers the Civil Liability Indexation Notice 2026 fixes for the
-- 2026-2027 financial year and the rules of the Civil Liability Regulation 2025, schedule 2, that bound an injury scale
-- value.
--
-- SEAT: the injured person who claims personal injury damages (or, for a death, those who claim for it). "What are my
-- general damages for this injury scale value?", "What is the most I can get for lost earnings, or lost super?", "Can I
-- claim for the care my family gives me, or for the care I can no longer give them?", "Can I claim for loss of
-- consortium?", "Will I get interest?", "Can I get exemplary damages?", "Can a mitigation notice cut my damages?".
--
-- The injury scale value is assessed by the court under the Regulation's schedules 3 to 5 (ranges for some 150
-- injuries, a psychiatric rating scale); it is asked as a number. Average weekly earnings (Sch 2 of the Act, declared by
-- the Australian Statistician) and the 10 year Treasury bond rate (s 60(3), published by the Reserve Bank) are not
-- deposited: asked. Amounts fixed by notice for a financial year other than 2026-2027 are not deposited: the answer is
-- NOTHING ("not covered") there.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Civil Liability Act 2003: damages for personal injury`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 61, 62 and the Indexation Notice 2026 s 4 -- general damages`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 61(1)(a) "the injured person's total general damages must be assigned a numerical value (injury scale value) on a
-- scale running from 0 to 100". Regulation sch 2 s 14 "An ISV assessed by a court must be a whole number."
GIVEN `the value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 61 -- a possible injury scale value` `the value` IF
        (`the value` AT LEAST 0)
    AND (`the value` AT MOST 100)
    AND (`the value` EQUALS FLOOR `the value`)

-- Indexation Notice 2026 s 4, table, for the 2026-2027 financial year: the base amount (column 2) plus the variable
-- amount (column 3) for each band of injury scale values. Hand-copied from the deposited notice, item by item.
GIVEN `the value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`notice 2026 s 4 -- general damages for an injury scale value` `the value` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the value` AT MOST 5  THEN `the value` TIMES 2010
        IF `the value` AT MOST 10 THEN 10050 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 5) TIMES 2360)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 15 THEN 21850 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 10) TIMES 2770)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 20 THEN 35700 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 15) TIMES 3150)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 25 THEN 51450 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 20) TIMES 3530)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 30 THEN 69100 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 25) TIMES 3960)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 35 THEN 88900 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 30) TIMES 4350)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 40 THEN 110650 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 35) TIMES 4740)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 50 THEN 134350 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 40) TIMES 5110)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 60 THEN 185450 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 50) TIMES 5450)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 70 THEN 239950 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 60) TIMES 5790)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 80 THEN 297850 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 70) TIMES 6190)
        IF `the value` AT MOST 90 THEN 359750 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 80) TIMES 6540)
        OTHERWISE 425150 PLUS ((`the value` MINUS 90) TIMES 6900)

-- s 62(1) "For an injury arising after 1 December 2002, general damages must be calculated under this section."
-- s 62(4) "The general damages for an injury are the sum of the following amounts, fixed by the notice under subsection
-- (2), for the period within which the injury arose". Only the notice for 2026-2027 is deposited: NOTHING for an injury
-- arising in another financial year, or on or before 1 December 2002 (s 62 does not apply: common law, not this Act),
-- or for a value that is not a whole number from 0 to 100.
GIVEN `the value` IS A NUMBER
      `the day the injury arose` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 62 -- general damages` `the value` `the day the injury arose` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the date` `the day the injury arose` `falls on or after` (`the calendar day` 2 12 2002)) THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (`the day falls in the 2026-2027 financial year` `the day the injury arose`) THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (`s 61 -- a possible injury scale value` `the value`) THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST (`notice 2026 s 4 -- general damages for an injury scale value` `the value`)

@export What are my general damages (for pain and suffering, loss of amenities and expectation of life, and disfigurement) for the injury scale value the court assesses (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 61, 62; Civil Liability Indexation Notice 2026 s 4)? The court gives your injury a whole-number value from 0 to 100 under the Civil Liability Regulation 2025; the dollar amount is fixed by the Minister's notice for the financial year in which the injury arose. Only the notice for 2026-2027 (injuries arising 1 July 2026 to 30 June 2027) is held here: for other years, and before 2 December 2002, the answer is "not covered".
GIVEN `injury scale value` IS A NUMBER @desc The injury scale value (ISV) the court assesses, or you expect it to assess, for your injuries: a whole number from 0 to 100
      `day injury arose` IS A DATE @desc The day your injury arose, as an ISO date such as 2026-08-14
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What are my general damages` MEANS
    `s 62 -- general damages` `injury scale value` `day injury arose`

-- Regulation sch 2 s 4: for multiple injuries the court may go above the maximum ISV for the dominant injury, but "(a)
-- must not be more than 100; and (b) should rarely be more than 25% higher than the maximum dominant ISV." "(4) If the
-- increase is more than 25% of the maximum dominant ISV, the court must give detailed written reasons for the
-- increase." s 61(2) of the Act: a value above or below those for similar injuries needs the factors stated.
DECLARE `How the court must explain an uplift for multiple injuries` IS ONE OF
    `no uplift: the value is not above the maximum for the dominant injury`
    `an uplift of no more than 25%: the court states the factors that justify it (Act s 61(2))`
    `an uplift of more than 25%: the court must give detailed written reasons (Regulation sch 2 s 4(4)), and should rarely do this`
    `not possible: an injury scale value cannot be more than 100 (Regulation sch 2 s 4(3)(a))`

GIVEN `the value` IS A NUMBER
      `the dominant maximum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `How the court must explain an uplift for multiple injuries`
`reg sch 2 s 4 -- uplift for multiple injuries` `the value` `the dominant maximum` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the value` GREATER THAN 100 THEN `not possible: an injury scale value cannot be more than 100 (Regulation sch 2 s 4(3)(a))`
        IF `the value` AT MOST `the dominant maximum` THEN `no uplift: the value is not above the maximum for the dominant injury`
        IF (`the value` MINUS `the dominant maximum`) GREATER THAN (`the dominant maximum` TIMES 25%) THEN `an uplift of more than 25%: the court must give detailed written reasons (Regulation sch 2 s 4(4)), and should rarely do this`
        OTHERWISE `an uplift of no more than 25%: the court states the factors that justify it (Act s 61(2))`

@export I have several injuries. If the court puts my injury scale value above the top of the range for my worst (dominant) injury, what must it do (Civil Liability Regulation 2025 sch 2 s 4; Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 61(2))? The value can never exceed 100, should rarely be more than 25% above the dominant injury's maximum, and an increase of more than 25% needs detailed written reasons.
GIVEN `assessed value` IS A NUMBER @desc The injury scale value the court assesses for all your injuries together
      `dominant injury maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc The highest injury scale value in the Regulation's range (schedule 3) for your dominant injury
GIVETH A `How the court must explain an uplift for multiple injuries`
`How must the court explain an uplift for multiple injuries` MEANS
    `reg sch 2 s 4 -- uplift for multiple injuries` `assessed value` `dominant injury maximum`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 54-57 -- loss of earnings and superannuation`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 54(1), (2): "the maximum award a court may make is ... an amount equal to the present value of 3 times average
-- weekly earnings per week for each week of the period of loss of earnings." (3) "present value means the value when
-- the award is made." s 55(4): the same limit applies where earnings cannot be precisely calculated. Average weekly
-- earnings (Sch 2: Queensland full-time adult persons ordinary time earnings declared by the Australian Statistician
-- most recently before the financial year) is asked. For past weeks the present value is taken as the weekly limit
-- itself; for future weeks the person gives the present value of $1 a week over the future period, at the prescribed
-- discount rate (s 57; fork F4).
GIVEN `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
      `past weeks` IS A NUMBER
      `future weeks at present value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 54 -- the limit on damages for loss of earnings` `average weekly earnings` `past weeks` `future weeks at present value` MEANS
    (3 TIMES `average weekly earnings`) TIMES (`past weeks` PLUS `future weeks at present value`)

@export What is the most a court can award me for loss of earnings, including in a claim by dependants for a death (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 54, 55)? The limit is the present value of 3 times average weekly earnings for each week of the period of loss. For weeks already past this is 3 times average weekly earnings a week; for future weeks it is discounted to present value.
GIVEN `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Average weekly earnings in dollars: Queensland full-time adult persons ordinary time earnings, as most recently declared by the Australian Statistician before the start of the financial year in which the award is made
      `past weeks of loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of weeks of loss of earnings up to the award
      `future weeks present value` IS A NUMBER @desc For the future period of loss: the present value, at the prescribed discount rate (5% unless a regulation under the Civil Proceedings Act 2011 sets another), of $1 a week for each future week of loss; give the plain number of future weeks if you want the undiscounted figure
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most I can get for loss of earnings` MEANS
    `s 54 -- the limit on damages for loss of earnings` `average weekly earnings` `past weeks of loss` `future weeks present value`

-- s 56(1) "The maximum amount of damages that may be awarded to an employee for economic loss due to the loss of
-- employer superannuation contributions is the relevant percentage of damages payable ... for the deprivation or
-- impairment of the earning capacity on which the entitlement to the contributions is based." (2) the relevant
-- percentage is the minimum a written law requires to be paid as employer contributions (borrowed: asked).
GIVEN `damages for earning capacity` IS A NUMBER
      `the minimum contribution percentage` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 56 -- the limit on damages for lost superannuation` `damages for earning capacity` `the minimum contribution percentage` MEANS
    `damages for earning capacity` TIMES (`the minimum contribution percentage` DIVIDED BY 100)

@export What is the most a court can award me, as an employee, for lost employer superannuation contributions (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 56)? It is the minimum percentage of earnings a law requires employers to contribute, applied to the damages for the loss of earning capacity on which the contributions were based.
GIVEN `damages for lost earning capacity` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages, in dollars, awarded for the loss or impairment of the earning capacity on which your employer superannuation contributions were based
      `minimum contribution percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum percentage of earnings a written law requires your employer to contribute to superannuation for you (for example the superannuation guarantee rate), as a number such as 12
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most I can get for lost superannuation` MEANS
    `s 56 -- the limit on damages for lost superannuation` `damages for lost earning capacity` `minimum contribution percent`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 58 and the Indexation Notice 2026 s 3 -- loss of consortium or servitium`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 58(1) "A court must not award damages for loss of consortium or loss of servitium unless-- (a) the injured person
-- died as a result of injuries suffered; or (b) general damages for the injured person are assessed (before allowing
-- for contributory negligence) at the amount fixed by the Minister, by written notice under section 75, for this
-- provision, or more." Notice 2026 s 3: $59,300 for the 2026-2027 financial year. Which financial year's amount applies
-- is not said: the year the damages are assessed is taken (fork F2). NOTHING-kind constructor for another year.
`notice 2026 s 3 -- the consortium threshold` MEANS 59300

DECLARE `Can damages for loss of consortium or servitium be awarded` IS ONE OF
    `yes, s 58(1) allows it: the injured person died as a result of the injuries (s 58(1)(a))`
    `yes, s 58(1) allows it: general damages, before contributory negligence, are at least the amount fixed for the year ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 58(1)(b))`
    `no: the injured person did not die and general damages are below the amount fixed for the year ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 58(1))`
    `not covered: the amount fixed for the financial year of the assessment is not held here (only 2026-2027)`

GIVEN `the injured person died of the injuries` IS A BOOLEAN
      `general damages before contributory negligence` IS A NUMBER
      `the day damages are assessed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Can damages for loss of consortium or servitium be awarded`
`s 58(1) -- the consortium threshold met` `the injured person died of the injuries` `general damages before contributory negligence` `the day damages are assessed` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the injured person died of the injuries` THEN `yes, s 58(1) allows it: the injured person died as a result of the injuries (s 58(1)(a))`
        IF NOT (`the day falls in the 2026-2027 financial year` `the day damages are assessed`) THEN `not covered: the amount fixed for the financial year of the assessment is not held here (only 2026-2027)`
        IF `general damages before contributory negligence` AT LEAST `notice 2026 s 3 -- the consortium threshold` THEN `yes, s 58(1) allows it: general damages, before contributory negligence, are at least the amount fixed for the year ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 58(1)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `no: the injured person did not die and general damages are below the amount fixed for the year ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 58(1))`

@export Can damages be awarded for loss of consortium (a spouse's loss of the injured person's companionship and services) or loss of servitium (an employer's loss of their services) (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 58(1); Civil Liability Indexation Notice 2026 s 3)? Only if the injured person died of the injuries, or their general damages, before any reduction for contributory negligence, are at least the amount the Minister fixes for the year: $59,300 for 2026-2027.
GIVEN `died of the injuries` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injured person died as a result of the injuries
      `general damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The general damages assessed for the injured person, in dollars, before any reduction for contributory negligence
      `day assessed` IS A DATE @desc The day the court assesses the damages, as an ISO date
GIVETH A `Can damages for loss of consortium or servitium be awarded`
`Can I claim for loss of consortium or servitium` MEANS
    `s 58(1) -- the consortium threshold met` `died of the injuries` `general damages` `day assessed`

-- s 58(2), (3): damages for loss of servitium must not be assessed above "3 times average weekly earnings per week".
GIVEN `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 58(3) -- the weekly limit for loss of servitium` `average weekly earnings` MEANS 3 TIMES `average weekly earnings`

@export What is the most per week that a court can assess for loss of servitium, an employer's loss of an injured employee's services (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 58(2), (3))? It is 3 times average weekly earnings per week.
GIVEN `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Average weekly earnings in dollars: Queensland full-time adult persons ordinary time earnings, as most recently declared by the Australian Statistician before the financial year
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the weekly limit for loss of servitium` MEANS
    `s 58(3) -- the weekly limit for loss of servitium` `average weekly earnings`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 59 -- gratuitous care given to me`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 59(1) "Damages for gratuitous services provided to an injured person are not to be awarded unless-- (a) the
-- services are necessary; and (b) the need for the services arises solely out of the injury ...; and (c) the services
-- are provided, or are to be provided-- (i) for at least 6 hours per week; and (ii) for at least 6 months." (2) not if
-- services of the same kind were provided before the breach of duty. (3) offsetting benefits and time in hospital are
-- taken into account in the amount. s 60(1)(b): no interest on these damages.
DECLARE `Can I claim for care given to me free` IS ONE OF
    `yes, s 59 allows damages for the gratuitous services (without interest, s 60(1)(b)); the amount allows for any benefit to the carer and time I am in hospital or another institution (s 59(3))`
    `no: the services are not necessary (s 59(1)(a))`
    `no: the need for the services does not arise solely out of the injury (s 59(1)(b))`
    `no: the services are not provided for at least 6 hours a week (s 59(1)(c)(i))`
    `no: the services are not provided for at least 6 months (s 59(1)(c)(ii))`
    `no: gratuitous services of the same kind were already being provided to me before the breach of duty (s 59(2))`

GIVEN `necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `solely out of the injury` IS A BOOLEAN
      `hours a week` IS A NUMBER
      `months` IS A NUMBER
      `the same kind before the breach` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I claim for care given to me free`
`s 59 -- gratuitous services to the injured person` `necessary` `solely out of the injury` `hours a week` `months` `the same kind before the breach` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`necessary`) THEN `no: the services are not necessary (s 59(1)(a))`
        IF NOT (`solely out of the injury`) THEN `no: the need for the services does not arise solely out of the injury (s 59(1)(b))`
        IF `hours a week` LESS THAN 6 THEN `no: the services are not provided for at least 6 hours a week (s 59(1)(c)(i))`
        IF `months` LESS THAN 6 THEN `no: the services are not provided for at least 6 months (s 59(1)(c)(ii))`
        IF `the same kind before the breach` THEN `no: gratuitous services of the same kind were already being provided to me before the breach of duty (s 59(2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes, s 59 allows damages for the gratuitous services (without interest, s 60(1)(b)); the amount allows for any benefit to the carer and time I am in hospital or another institution (s 59(3))`

@export Can I claim damages for the care and help family or friends give me free because of my injury (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 59)? Only if the services are necessary, the need arises solely from the injury, and they are (or will be) provided for at least 6 hours a week for at least 6 months, and not if services of the same kind were already being given to you before the injury. No interest is awarded on them.
GIVEN `necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the services are necessary
      `need solely from the injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the need for the services arises solely out of the injury you claim for
      `hours per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the services are, or will be, provided
      `months provided` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many months the services are, or will be, provided
      `same services before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether gratuitous services of the same kind were being provided to you before the breach of duty happened
GIVETH A `Can I claim for care given to me free`
`Can I claim for care given to me free` MEANS
    `s 59 -- gratuitous services to the injured person` `necessary` `need solely from the injury` `hours per week` `months provided` `same services before`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 59A-59D, 85 -- loss of my capacity to care for others (section 59A damages)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 59A(2): the court may award section 59A damages only if satisfied of all of: (a) I died of the injuries, or general
-- damages (before contributory negligence) are "at the amount prescribed under section 58, or more" (taken as the s 58
-- amount fixed by notice; finding 2); (b) at the relevant time the recipient lived at my usual residence, or was my
-- unborn child; (c) before then I provided the services (or, for an unborn child, would have); (d) the recipient is or
-- will be incapable of performing them because of age or physical or mental incapacity; (e) there is a reasonable
-- expectation that but for the injury I would have provided them for at least 6 hours a week for at least 6 months;
-- (f) there will be a need for them for those hours and that period, and the need is reasonable.
-- s 59A(3), (4): if only (e) and (f) fail because the recipient was in respite accommodation or care, the court may
-- still award them if it considers it reasonable.
-- s 59B: (1) not if the recipient is outside s 59A(2)(b); (2) not to the extent s 59 damages for care given to me also
-- give the recipient those services; (3) not to the extent a motor accident insurer pays for rehabilitation services
-- that do so; (4) not if the recipient has already recovered damages for the loss.
-- s 85: for a dust-related claim, whatever the date of the breach (unless settled, discontinued or decided before the
-- 2010 amending Act's assent: asked); for another claim, only for a breach on or after 1 July 2010.
DECLARE `Can I claim for the care I can no longer give` IS ONE OF
    `yes, the court may award section 59A damages if satisfied (s 59A(2)); they are assessed on my life expectancy before the injury, and general damages do not also cover this loss (s 59C)`
    `yes, the court may award section 59A damages because respite accommodation or care explains the shorter hours or period, if it considers this reasonable (s 59A(3), (4))`
    `no: for a claim that is not dust-related, section 59A damages are only for a breach of duty on or after 1 July 2010 (s 85(3))`
    `no: the dust-related claim was settled, discontinued or decided before the 2010 amendments (s 85(2))`
    `no: I did not die of the injuries, and general damages are below the amount for s 58 ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 59A(2)(a))`
    `not covered: the s 58 amount for the financial year of the assessment is not held here (only 2026-2027)`
    `no: the recipient did not live at my usual residence and was not my unborn child at the relevant time (ss 59A(2)(b), 59B(1))`
    `no: I did not provide the services to the recipient before the injury (s 59A(2)(c))`
    `no: the recipient is not incapable of performing the services because of age or incapacity (s 59A(2)(d))`
    `no: the hours, period or reasonable need required by s 59A(2)(e), (f) are not met`
    `no, to that extent: care given to me under s 59 also provides the recipient with those services (s 59B(2))`
    `no, to that extent: a motor accident insurer pays for rehabilitation services that provide the recipient with those services (s 59B(3))`
    `no: the recipient has already recovered damages for this loss (s 59B(4))`

DECLARE `Facts for section 59A damages` HAS
    `dust-related claim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `dust claim concluded before 2010` IS A BOOLEAN
    `day of the breach` IS A DATE
    `died of the injuries` IS A BOOLEAN
    `general damages before contributory negligence` IS A NUMBER
    `day assessed` IS A DATE
    `recipient lived with me or was my unborn child` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I provided the services before` IS A BOOLEAN
    `recipient incapable through age or incapacity` IS A BOOLEAN
    `six hours and six months expected and needed` IS A BOOLEAN
    `respite care explains the shortfall and award is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
    `s 59 care also provides them` IS A BOOLEAN
    `motor accident rehabilitation also provides them` IS A BOOLEAN
    `recipient already recovered` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `f` IS A `Facts for section 59A damages`
GIVETH A `Can I claim for the care I can no longer give`
`ss 59A, 59B, 85 -- section 59A damages` `f` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`f`'s `dust-related claim`)) AND (`the date` (`f`'s `day of the breach`) `falls before` (`the calendar day` 1 7 2010))
        THEN `no: for a claim that is not dust-related, section 59A damages are only for a breach of duty on or after 1 July 2010 (s 85(3))`
        IF `f`'s `dust-related claim` AND `f`'s `dust claim concluded before 2010`
        THEN `no: the dust-related claim was settled, discontinued or decided before the 2010 amendments (s 85(2))`
        IF NOT (`f`'s `recipient lived with me or was my unborn child`)
        THEN `no: the recipient did not live at my usual residence and was not my unborn child at the relevant time (ss 59A(2)(b), 59B(1))`
        IF `f`'s `recipient already recovered`
        THEN `no: the recipient has already recovered damages for this loss (s 59B(4))`
        IF `the threshold answer` EQUALS `not covered: the s 58 amount for the financial year of the assessment is not held here (only 2026-2027)`
        THEN `not covered: the s 58 amount for the financial year of the assessment is not held here (only 2026-2027)`
        IF `the threshold answer` EQUALS `no: I did not die of the injuries, and general damages are below the amount for s 58 ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 59A(2)(a))`
        THEN `no: I did not die of the injuries, and general damages are below the amount for s 58 ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 59A(2)(a))`
        IF NOT (`f`'s `I provided the services before`)
        THEN `no: I did not provide the services to the recipient before the injury (s 59A(2)(c))`
        IF NOT (`f`'s `recipient incapable through age or incapacity`)
        THEN `no: the recipient is not incapable of performing the services because of age or incapacity (s 59A(2)(d))`
        IF `f`'s `s 59 care also provides them`
        THEN `no, to that extent: care given to me under s 59 also provides the recipient with those services (s 59B(2))`
        IF `f`'s `motor accident rehabilitation also provides them`
        THEN `no, to that extent: a motor accident insurer pays for rehabilitation services that provide the recipient with those services (s 59B(3))`
        IF `f`'s `six hours and six months expected and needed`
        THEN `yes, the court may award section 59A damages if satisfied (s 59A(2)); they are assessed on my life expectancy before the injury, and general damages do not also cover this loss (s 59C)`
        IF `f`'s `respite care explains the shortfall and award is reasonable`
        THEN `yes, the court may award section 59A damages because respite accommodation or care explains the shorter hours or period, if it considers this reasonable (s 59A(3), (4))`
        OTHERWISE `no: the hours, period or reasonable need required by s 59A(2)(e), (f) are not met`
    WHERE
        `the threshold answer` MEANS
            CONSIDER `s 58(1) -- the consortium threshold met` (`f`'s `died of the injuries`) (`f`'s `general damages before contributory negligence`) (`f`'s `day assessed`)
            WHEN `yes, s 58(1) allows it: the injured person died as a result of the injuries (s 58(1)(a))` THEN `yes, the court may award section 59A damages if satisfied (s 59A(2)); they are assessed on my life expectancy before the injury, and general damages do not also cover this loss (s 59C)`
            WHEN `yes, s 58(1) allows it: general damages, before contributory negligence, are at least the amount fixed for the year ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 58(1)(b))` THEN `yes, the court may award section 59A damages if satisfied (s 59A(2)); they are assessed on my life expectancy before the injury, and general damages do not also cover this loss (s 59C)`
            WHEN `no: the injured person did not die and general damages are below the amount fixed for the year ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 58(1))` THEN `no: I did not die of the injuries, and general damages are below the amount for s 58 ($59,300 in 2026-2027) (s 59A(2)(a))`
            WHEN `not covered: the amount fixed for the financial year of the assessment is not held here (only 2026-2027)` THEN `not covered: the s 58 amount for the financial year of the assessment is not held here (only 2026-2027)`

@export My injury means I can no longer give unpaid domestic care to someone in my household (for example a child or an elderly parent). Can I claim "section 59A damages" for that lost capacity (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 59A-59D, 85)? The court may award them only if you died of the injuries or your general damages are at least the s 58 amount ($59,300 for 2026-2027); the person you cared for lived at your usual residence (or was your unborn child) and cannot do it themselves because of age or incapacity; you gave the care before; and you would have given it, and it will be needed, for at least 6 hours a week for at least 6 months (or respite care explains the shortfall). They are not awarded to the extent other damages or insurer-paid rehabilitation already provide the care, or if the person cared for has already recovered for it. For claims that are not for a dust disease, the breach must be on or after 1 July 2010. These damages are available even for dust disease and tobacco claims, which the rest of the Act does not cover.
GIVEN `dust-related claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for personal injury resulting from a dust-related condition (for example asbestosis, mesothelioma or silicosis)
      `dust claim concluded before 2010` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a dust-related claim only: whether, before the Civil Liability and Other Legislation Amendment Act 2010 received assent, it was settled, or a proceeding for it was discontinued or decided by judgment
      `day of the breach` IS A DATE @desc The day the breach of duty happened, as an ISO date
      `died of the injuries` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injured person died as a result of the injuries
      `general damages` IS A NUMBER @desc General damages assessed for the injured person, in dollars, before any reduction for contributory negligence
      `day assessed` IS A DATE @desc The day the court assesses the damages, as an ISO date
      `recipient lived with me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when the injury happened (or when its nature and extent became known, if its symptoms were not immediately apparent), the person you cared for lived at your usual residence or was your unborn child
      `I provided services before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether before then you provided the domestic services to that person (or, for an unborn child, would have once born)
      `recipient incapable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that person is, or will be, incapable of performing the services themselves because of their age or physical or mental incapacity
      `six hours six months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, but for the injury, you would reasonably be expected to have provided the services for at least 6 hours a week for at least 6 months, and there will be a reasonable need for them for those hours and that period
      `respite explains shortfall` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If not: whether that is only because the person was in accommodation provided by another parent, or in respite or other care given because they are aged, frail or disabled to give a break from usual care arrangements, and the court considers an award reasonable
      `s 59 care also provides them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether damages you can recover for care given to you (s 59) would also provide the person with the domestic services you can no longer give
      `MAI rehabilitation provides them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, for a motor accident injury, an insurer has paid or must pay for rehabilitation services under the Motor Accident Insurance Act 1994 s 51 that also provide the person with those services
      `recipient already recovered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you cared for has already recovered damages for the loss caused by your loss of capacity
GIVETH A `Can I claim for the care I can no longer give`
`Can I claim for the care I can no longer give` MEANS
    `ss 59A, 59B, 85 -- section 59A damages`
        (`Facts for section 59A damages` WITH
            `dust-related claim` IS `dust-related claim`
            `dust claim concluded before 2010` IS `dust claim concluded before 2010`
            `day of the breach` IS `day of the breach`
            `died of the injuries` IS `died of the injuries`
            `general damages before contributory negligence` IS `general damages`
            `day assessed` IS `day assessed`
            `recipient lived with me or was my unborn child` IS `recipient lived with me`
            `I provided the services before` IS `I provided services before`
            `recipient incapable through age or incapacity` IS `recipient incapable`
            `six hours and six months expected and needed` IS `six hours six months`
            `respite care explains the shortfall and award is reasonable` IS `respite explains shortfall`
            `s 59 care also provides them` IS `s 59 care also provides them`
            `motor accident rehabilitation also provides them` IS `MAI rehabilitation provides them`
            `recipient already recovered` IS `recipient already recovered`)

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 60 -- interest`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 60(1) no interest on general damages or on damages for gratuitous services provided to the injured person.
-- (2) interest on damages for past monetary loss must not be more than interest at the appropriate rate (the 10 year
-- Treasury bond rate, RBA table F2, at the start of the quarter of the award: asked) and must be related to the period.
DECLARE `Kind of damages` IS ONE OF
    `general damages`
    `damages for gratuitous services provided to me`
    `damages for past monetary loss (for example lost wages or medical expenses already paid)`
    `damages for future loss`

DECLARE `Can I get interest` IS ONE OF
    `no: a court cannot order interest on general damages (s 60(1)(a))`
    `no: a court cannot order interest on damages for gratuitous services provided to me (s 60(1)(b))`
    `yes, limited: no more than interest at the 10 year Treasury bond rate at the start of the quarter of the award, related to the period over which the loss was incurred (s 60(2), (3))`
    `s 60 does not deal with interest on damages for future loss`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of damages`
GIVETH A `Can I get interest`
`s 60 -- interest on damages` `the kind` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `general damages` THEN `no: a court cannot order interest on general damages (s 60(1)(a))`
    WHEN `damages for gratuitous services provided to me` THEN `no: a court cannot order interest on damages for gratuitous services provided to me (s 60(1)(b))`
    WHEN `damages for past monetary loss (for example lost wages or medical expenses already paid)` THEN `yes, limited: no more than interest at the 10 year Treasury bond rate at the start of the quarter of the award, related to the period over which the loss was incurred (s 60(2), (3))`
    WHEN `damages for future loss` THEN `s 60 does not deal with interest on damages for future loss`

@export Can I get interest on this part of my damages (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 60)? There is no interest on general damages or on damages for free care given to you; interest on past monetary loss is limited to the 10 year Treasury bond rate at the start of the quarter of the award, over the period of the loss.
GIVEN `kind of damages` IS A `Kind of damages` @desc Which part of your damages: general damages; damages for gratuitous services provided to you; damages for past monetary loss; or damages for future loss
GIVETH A `Can I get interest`
`Can I get interest on these damages` MEANS
    `s 60 -- interest on damages` `kind of damages`

-- s 60, the example: for past monetary loss incurred at a uniform rate over a period, "A = am/100 x p x 0.5", where a
-- is the percentage rate (no more than the appropriate rate), m the aggregate loss and p the period in years.
GIVEN `the rate` IS A NUMBER, `the loss` IS A NUMBER, `the years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 60 example -- interest on a loss incurred evenly` `the rate` `the loss` `the years` MEANS
    ((`the rate` TIMES `the loss`) DIVIDED BY 100) TIMES `the years` TIMES 0.5

@export How much interest might I get on past expenses (or other past monetary loss) that built up evenly over time (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 60, the example)? The example in the Act computes A = a x m / 100 x p x 0.5, where a is the interest rate (at most the 10 year Treasury bond rate at the start of the quarter of the award), m the total loss and p the period in years.
GIVEN `rate percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The interest rate in percent the court uses, at most the 10 year Treasury bond rate published by the Reserve Bank (table F2) at the start of the quarter in which the award is made
      `total past loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The total past monetary loss in dollars (for example the medical expenses paid)
      `years` IS A NUMBER @desc The period over which the loss was incurred, in years
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much interest on past expenses incurred evenly` MEANS
    `s 60 example -- interest on a loss incurred evenly` `rate percent` `total past loss` `years`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 52 -- exemplary, punitive or aggravated damages`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 52(1) "A court can not award exemplary, punitive or aggravated damages in relation to a claim for personal injury
-- damages." (2) not if the act was "(a) an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury; or (b)
-- an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct."
DECLARE `What caused my injury` IS ONE OF
    `negligence or another act not done with intent to injure`
    `an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury`
    `an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct`

DECLARE `Can I get exemplary damages` IS ONE OF
    `no: a court cannot award exemplary, punitive or aggravated damages on a personal injury claim (s 52(1))`
    `s 52 does not prevent them: the injury was caused by an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury (s 52(2)(a)); the court decides`
    `s 52 does not prevent them: the injury was caused by an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct (s 52(2)(b)); the court decides`

GIVEN `the cause` IS A `What caused my injury`
GIVETH A `Can I get exemplary damages`
`s 52 -- exemplary damages` `the cause` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the cause`
    WHEN `negligence or another act not done with intent to injure` THEN `no: a court cannot award exemplary, punitive or aggravated damages on a personal injury claim (s 52(1))`
    WHEN `an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury` THEN `s 52 does not prevent them: the injury was caused by an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury (s 52(2)(a)); the court decides`
    WHEN `an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct` THEN `s 52 does not prevent them: the injury was caused by an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct (s 52(2)(b)); the court decides`

@export Can I get exemplary, punitive or aggravated damages for my personal injury (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 52)? Not on a personal injury claim, unless the injury was caused by an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury, or by an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct.
GIVEN `cause of injury` IS A `What caused my injury` @desc What caused your injury: negligence or another act not done with intent to injure; an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury; or an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct
GIVETH A `Can I get exemplary damages`
`Can I get exemplary damages` MEANS
    `s 52 -- exemplary damages` `cause of injury`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 52A-52C -- motor accident: participants in the national injury insurance scheme`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 52B(2): for personal injury from a motor accident, where the person is or was a participant in the insurance
-- scheme for a serious personal injury from it, "A court can not award damages in relation to the person's treatment,
-- care and support needs that-- (a) result from the personal injury; and (b) arise, or arose, while the person is, or
-- was, a participant". Subject to s 52C: on a claim against an insurer under the Motor Accident Insurance Act 1994, for
-- a lifetime participant, if the court decides the person was not contributorily negligent, or the reduction is less
-- than 50%, and the agency is liable under the National Injury Act s 42 to contribute, the court may award treatment,
-- care and support damages, without taking contributory negligence into account.
DECLARE `My place in the national injury insurance scheme` IS ONE OF
    `not a participant`
    `an interim participant (a participant, not a lifetime participant)`
    `a lifetime participant`

DECLARE `Can I get damages for treatment, care and support` IS ONE OF
    `s 52B does not apply: I am not, and was not, a participant in the scheme for a serious personal injury from the motor accident`
    `no: a court cannot award damages for treatment, care and support needs arising while I am or was a participant (s 52B(2)); the scheme meets them`
    `yes, the court may award treatment, care and support damages, without reducing them for contributory negligence (s 52C)`

GIVEN `my place` IS A `My place in the national injury insurance scheme`
      `a claim against a motor accident insurer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `contributory negligence percent` IS A NUMBER
      `the agency must contribute` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I get damages for treatment, care and support`
`ss 52B, 52C -- treatment, care and support damages` `my place` `a claim against a motor accident insurer` `contributory negligence percent` `the agency must contribute` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my place` EQUALS `not a participant`
        THEN `s 52B does not apply: I am not, and was not, a participant in the scheme for a serious personal injury from the motor accident`
        IF (`my place` EQUALS `a lifetime participant`) AND `a claim against a motor accident insurer` AND (`contributory negligence percent` LESS THAN 50) AND `the agency must contribute`
        THEN `yes, the court may award treatment, care and support damages, without reducing them for contributory negligence (s 52C)`
        OTHERWISE `no: a court cannot award damages for treatment, care and support needs arising while I am or was a participant (s 52B(2)); the scheme meets them`

@export I was seriously injured in a motor accident and I am in the National Injury Insurance Scheme, Queensland. Can I also get damages for my treatment, care and support needs (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 52A-52C)? Generally not for needs arising while you are a participant: the scheme meets them. But a lifetime participant claiming against a motor accident insurer may be awarded them, without any cut for contributory negligence, if the court finds no contributory negligence or a reduction of less than 50%, and the scheme's agency must contribute to the insurer's liability.
GIVEN `scheme place` IS A `My place in the national injury insurance scheme` @desc Whether you are, or were, a participant in the national injury insurance scheme, Queensland for a serious personal injury from the motor accident: not a participant; an interim participant; or a lifetime participant
      `claim against MAI insurer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is against an insurer under the Motor Accident Insurance Act 1994
      `contributory negligence percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which the court reduces your damages for contributory negligence (0 if none)
      `agency liable to contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the National Injury Insurance Agency, Queensland is liable under the National Injury Insurance Scheme (Queensland) Act 2016 s 42 to contribute to the insurer's liability for treatment, care and support damages
GIVETH A `Can I get damages for treatment, care and support`
`Can I get damages for treatment care and support` MEANS
    `ss 52B, 52C -- treatment, care and support damages` `scheme place` `claim against MAI insurer` `contributory negligence percent` `agency liable to contribute`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 53 -- a notice suggesting I mitigate my damages`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 53(1) a defendant not satisfied with what I have done to mitigate "may give the plaintiff written notice suggesting
-- specified action". (4) the court must consider whether I failed to take reasonable steps by not following it (or a
-- notice under the Personal Injuries Proceedings Act 2002 s 26), whether an offer to pay for treatment came with it,
-- and "reduce the plaintiff's damages to an appropriate extent ... if ... the court considers that the plaintiff's
-- failure to follow the suggestions was not reasonable." (5) not where leave to start the proceeding was given under
-- PIPA s 43(1) and not stayed because s 43(4) applied.
DECLARE `Can a mitigation notice cut my damages` IS ONE OF
    `no notice: s 53(4) does not apply, but my ordinary duty to mitigate remains (s 53(3))`
    `no cut under s 53: I followed the suggestions in the notice`
    `no cut under s 53: the court considers my failure to follow the suggestions was reasonable`
    `s 53(4) does not apply: leave to start my proceeding was given under the Personal Injuries Proceedings Act 2002 s 43(1) and not stayed because s 43(4) applied (s 53(5))`
    `yes: the court must reduce my damages to an appropriate extent, because my failure to follow the suggestions was not reasonable (s 53(4)(c))`

GIVEN `a notice was given` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I followed it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my failure was not reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `urgent leave not stayed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can a mitigation notice cut my damages`
`s 53 -- notice to mitigate` `a notice was given` `I followed it` `my failure was not reasonable` `urgent leave not stayed` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`a notice was given`) THEN `no notice: s 53(4) does not apply, but my ordinary duty to mitigate remains (s 53(3))`
        IF `urgent leave not stayed` THEN `s 53(4) does not apply: leave to start my proceeding was given under the Personal Injuries Proceedings Act 2002 s 43(1) and not stayed because s 43(4) applied (s 53(5))`
        IF `I followed it` THEN `no cut under s 53: I followed the suggestions in the notice`
        IF `my failure was not reasonable` THEN `yes: the court must reduce my damages to an appropriate extent, because my failure to follow the suggestions was not reasonable (s 53(4)(c))`
        OTHERWISE `no cut under s 53: the court considers my failure to follow the suggestions was reasonable`

@export The defendant sent me a written notice suggesting treatment, rehabilitation or a return to work. Can my damages be cut if I do not follow it (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 53)? The court must consider whether you failed to take reasonable steps by not following the suggestions (and whether the defendant offered to pay for suggested treatment), and must reduce your damages to an appropriate extent if it considers your failure was not reasonable. This does not apply where leave to start your proceeding was given urgently under the Personal Injuries Proceedings Act 2002 s 43(1) and not stayed because s 43(4) applied.
GIVEN `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant gave you written notice suggesting specified action to mitigate your damages (under s 53, or the Personal Injuries Proceedings Act 2002 s 26)
      `followed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you followed the suggestions
      `failure unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you did not: whether the court considers that, in all the circumstances, your failure was not reasonable (taking into account whether the defendant offered to pay for suggested treatment or therapy)
      `urgent leave not stayed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether leave to start your proceeding was given under the Personal Injuries Proceedings Act 2002 s 43(1) and the proceeding was not stayed because s 43(4) applied
GIVETH A `Can a mitigation notice cut my damages`
`Can a mitigation notice cut my damages` MEANS
    `s 53 -- notice to mitigate` `notice given` `followed it` `failure unreasonable` `urgent leave not stayed`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 63-67, 73 -- structured settlements and trial without a jury`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 64(2) "A court that decides to make an award for future loss (not including interest) of more than the amount
-- fixed by the Minister, by written notice under section 75, for this section must first notify all the parties to the
-- proceeding of the terms of the award it proposes to make." Notice 2026 s 5: $197,770 for 2026-2027 (the year the
-- court decides; fork F2).
`notice 2026 s 5 -- the structured settlement amount` MEANS 197770

DECLARE `Must the court tell me of the award first` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the award for future loss is more than the amount fixed for the year ($197,770 in 2026-2027), so the court must first notify the parties of its terms, to let us negotiate a structured settlement (s 64)`
    `no: the award for future loss is not more than the amount fixed for the year ($197,770 in 2026-2027) (s 64(2))`
    `not covered: the amount fixed for the financial year of the decision is not held here (only 2026-2027)`

GIVEN `the future loss award` IS A NUMBER
      `the day of the decision` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Must the court tell me of the award first`
`s 64 -- notice of a proposed award` `the future loss award` `the day of the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the day falls in the 2026-2027 financial year` `the day of the decision`) THEN `not covered: the amount fixed for the financial year of the decision is not held here (only 2026-2027)`
        IF `the future loss award` GREATER THAN `notice 2026 s 5 -- the structured settlement amount` THEN `yes: the award for future loss is more than the amount fixed for the year ($197,770 in 2026-2027), so the court must first notify the parties of its terms, to let us negotiate a structured settlement (s 64)`
        OTHERWISE `no: the award for future loss is not more than the amount fixed for the year ($197,770 in 2026-2027) (s 64(2))`

@export Must the court tell the parties what it proposes to award me before it makes the award, so we can negotiate a structured settlement (periodic payments instead of a lump sum) (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 63-65; Civil Liability Indexation Notice 2026 s 5)? Yes, if the award for future loss, not counting interest, is more than the amount fixed for the year: $197,770 for 2026-2027. The court may also approve a structured settlement the parties agree.
GIVEN `future loss award` IS A NUMBER @desc The award the court proposes for future loss (future economic loss, future general expenses and future medical expenses), in dollars, not counting interest
      `day of decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the court decides to make the award, as an ISO date
GIVETH A `Must the court tell me of the award first`
`Must the court tell me of the award first` MEANS
    `s 64 -- notice of a proposed award` `future loss award` `day of decision`

-- s 66 "A lawyer engaged by a plaintiff must advise the plaintiff, in writing, about the following if the plaintiff
-- proposes to negotiate a settlement of a claim for personal injury damages-- (a) the availability of structured
-- settlements; (b) the desirability of the plaintiff obtaining independent financial advice ...".
-- s 73 "A proceeding in a court based on a claim for personal injury damages must be decided by the court sitting
-- without a jury." (s 77, a transitional exception for jury trials already available under the Personal Injuries
-- Proceedings Act 2002 before 2003, is out.)
DECLARE `What I am owed in a settlement and at trial` HAS
    `my lawyer must advise me in writing about structured settlements and independent financial advice (s 66)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my claim is decided by a judge without a jury (s 73)` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `personal injury claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I have a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I propose to negotiate a settlement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What I am owed in a settlement and at trial`
`ss 66, 73 -- settlement advice and no jury` `personal injury claim` `I have a lawyer` `I propose to negotiate a settlement` MEANS
    `What I am owed in a settlement and at trial` WITH
        `my lawyer must advise me in writing about structured settlements and independent financial advice (s 66)` IS `personal injury claim` AND `I have a lawyer` AND `I propose to negotiate a settlement`
        `my claim is decided by a judge without a jury (s 73)` IS `personal injury claim`

@export If I settle or go to trial on my personal injury claim, must my lawyer advise me about structured settlements, and will a jury hear my case (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 66, 73)? A lawyer you engage must advise you in writing, if you propose to negotiate a settlement, about the availability of structured settlements and the desirability of getting independent financial advice about them and about a lump sum. A court proceeding on a claim for personal injury damages is decided by the court sitting without a jury.
GIVEN `personal injury claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for personal injury damages (damages for death or injury)
      `lawyer engaged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have engaged a lawyer for the claim
      `propose to settle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you propose to negotiate a settlement of the claim
GIVETH A `What I am owed in a settlement and at trial`
`Must my lawyer advise me and will a jury hear my claim` MEANS
    `ss 66, 73 -- settlement advice and no jury` `personal injury claim` `lawyer engaged` `propose to settle`
